{
 "note": "A working library of the surfaces a building is actually made of, as PBR numbers. Say which of the three each number is, because they are not the same kind of thing: REFLECTANCE is the published typical value for the material class - the ranges lighting engineers have used for decades and that CIBSE and IESNA tabulate for daylight and interior lighting calculations - converted to a linear RGB triple at the stated hue. ROUGHNESS and METALNESS are a judgement about how a surface scatters, not a measurement: nobody publishes a GGX roughness for fair-faced concrete, and anyone who tells you they do has fitted one to a render they liked. IOR is the published refractive index where the material has one. Nothing here was measured by this program.",
 "reflectance": "`reflectance` is the LUMINOUS REFLECTANCE of the surface - the fraction of the light landing on it that comes back into the room. It is the number a daylight or interior-lighting calculation wants, and it is the published typical value for the material class, not a measurement made here. A fair-faced concrete is anywhere from 0.20 to 0.40 depending on the cement, the aggregate and the weather it cured in; this is the middle of that.",
 "version": 1,
