 "colour_note": "`colour` is NOT always the same quantity as `reflectance`, and conflating them is the commonest way a material library goes quietly wrong. For a DIELECTRIC - anything with metalness 0 and no transmission - the colour IS the diffuse albedo and its luminance equals the reflectance; the tests check that, for all 53 of them. For a METAL the colour is F0, the specular reflectance at normal incidence, which is what gives brass its yellow and is a different and larger number than what the panel sends back into the room. For a TRANSMISSIVE material the colour is the tint light picks up going through it, which for clear glass is almost white while its surface reflectance is 0.08. Both numbers are given for every entry so that the renderer can have the one it needs and a daylight calculation can have the other.",
 "roughness_note": "`roughness` and `metalness` are a JUDGEMENT about how a surface scatters, not a measurement. Nobody publishes a GGX roughness for board-marked concrete; anyone who gives you one has fitted it to a render they liked. They are a starting point to move, which is what the sliders are for.",
 "ior_note": "`ior` is the published refractive index where the material has one - 1.52 for soda-lime glass, 1.585 for polycarbonate, 1.40 for ETFE."
}